{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790375097,"nonce":"o9fU2S3R/QEKbmXNfrql+DxRsgw=","title":"MIR silently drops the rest of a function when an expression yields no value","message":"Probe (before the interpolation-inlining fix):\n\n    private abstract S(String) {\n      public function new(v:String) this = v;\n      public function m():String return '$this';\n    }\n    class R4 { static function main() { var s = new S(\"r\"); trace(\"[\" + s.m() + \"]\"); } }\n\n`s.m()` is inlined into main by try_inline_abstract_method; `this` inside the interpolation was lowered with plain lower_expression (unbound). RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR showed main as:\n\n    $3 = const \"[\"\n    $4 = const \"\"\n    ret void\n\nEverything after the failed operand vanished -- no W0020, no error, exit 0, the trace never prints. Any lowering that returns None mid-function turns into a silently truncated function instead of a trap stub, so a compiler gap scores as WRONG_ANSWER / NO_OUTPUT rather than an uncompiled crash.\n\nFix direction: when an expression statement's lowering yields None where a value was required (binary operand, call argument), record a lowering error so the function is trap-stubbed with a reason, as other value_map misses already are.\n\nThe specific trigger (interpolation inside an inlined abstract method) is fixed separately; inline_expression_deep still falls back to lower_expression for FunctionCall/Block/Var/lambda bodies, which leave `this` unbound the same way.","files":null}]}